1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,755
SIDS are smaller datasets that live in the SIDS folder as CSV files

2
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:14,835
Now let's make sure that if you open your dbt project yaml file

3
00:00:14,835 --> 00:00:18,997
that your SIDS path it is set to the SIDS folder

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,948
If you have a data folder here please rename it to SIDS folder

5
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:26,276
And what we are going to do is that we take a CSV file

6
00:00:26,276 --> 00:00:29,166
which you can get from the class resources

7
00:00:29,166 --> 00:00:33,593
but I can also show you a direct way of importing them

8
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:37,210
We will copy that to the SIDS folder

9
00:00:37,210 --> 00:00:44,359
and then upload it to Snowflake through dbt So simple

10
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:50,573
So let's go and populate our SIDS folder with the CSV file

11
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:56,026
I have a CSV file here and let me go to my terminal

12
00:00:56,026 --> 00:01:02,400
at our S 3 bucket and it's called SIDS Fullmoon Dates

13
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:10,520
I will now go and use this curl command to copy it straight to the SIDS folder

14
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:15,300
If you don't have a curl command simply take it from the class resources

15
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:21,837
or download it from this URL and copy it to the SIDS folder manually

16
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,436
So now it must be here

17
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:32,023
So this is SIDS Fullmoon Dates and here are all the dates for the last 10 years or so

18
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:35,320
where there was a full moon

19
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:45,047
Now what we are after is to check if people give more negative results after full moons

20
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:48,518
So is it true that full moon affects your sleep

21
00:01:48,518 --> 00:01:53,960
and probably no it affects your mood We see this later

22
00:01:53,960 --> 00:02:00,680
Let's copy this CSV to Snowflake but before we do that just one more remark

23
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:03,406
You see that this CSV is a single column CSV

24
00:02:03,406 --> 00:02:08,760
but you don't necessarily need to work with single column CSVs when you work with SIDS

25
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:15,276
We just have a single column CSV here as a simple example

26
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:21,826
If you want to go and work with a seed you simply say dbtseed

27
00:02:21,826 --> 00:02:28,679
and the seeds will be populated to Snowflake So simple

28
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:33,555
Ok so it says dev SIDS Fullmoon Dates is getting imported

29
00:02:33,555 --> 00:02:37,600
and the import completed successfully

30
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:43,683
So let me go to Snowflake and refresh

31
00:02:43,683 --> 00:02:53,280
and here in the tables you will see that I have a seed Fullmoon Date

32
00:02:53,280 --> 00:03:00,720
If we take a look I can see that I have the data in here

33
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:05,016
You also see that the Fullmoon Date column itself

34
00:03:05,016 --> 00:03:07,522
it is a date type column

35
00:03:07,522 --> 00:03:12,399
and it's a date type column because dbt internally makes a pass

36
00:03:12,399 --> 00:03:17,160
on your CSV and figures out the schema automatically

37
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:20,506
So now we are ready to implement our fourth layer

38
00:03:20,506 --> 00:03:22,290
which will be our mart layer

39
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:26,770
The mart layer usually hoists those tables and views

40
00:03:26,770 --> 00:03:29,751
which are accessible by the itools

41
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:33,057
So let's create our mart folder

42
00:03:33,057 --> 00:03:38,550
and in this mart folder I'm going to create a new file

43
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:45,395
which is called mart Fullmoon Reviews sql

44
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:49,480
You can get this file from the class resources

45
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:54,632
I want to make sure that this table will be a table

46
00:03:54,632 --> 00:03:59,712
and here in this table I want to prove or refute

47
00:03:59,712 --> 00:04:05,560
the hypothesis that Fullmoon has a bad effect on people's moods

48
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:10,750
So what I will do is that I will reference the reviews

49
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:14,571
and Fullmoon dates and join them together

50
00:04:14,571 --> 00:04:24,551
so I'm matching every Fullmoon date with the review date of the upcoming day

51
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,808
And I will add this property

52
00:04:26,808 --> 00:04:35,200
if for a certain review the previous night was a Fullmoon or not

53
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:43,000
So the idea here is that we reference the seeds directly

54
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:47,680
as if it was a single model

55
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:54,265
So let's run our dbtick Ok

56
00:04:54,265 --> 00:04:58,315
and there you see I have the dev mart Fullmoon Reviews table created

57
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:04,632
So if I go to snowflake hopefully if I make a refresh

58
00:05:04,632 --> 00:05:09,360
then in tables now I have mart Fullmoon reviews

59
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:13,312
Perfect It has all the review columns

60
00:05:13,312 --> 00:05:16,633
and also if it's a Fullmoon or not

61
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:24,878
We can check the results later
